From 3cdb6076d6de8696896ec9e962bab19425c8fd39 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: lucascouts <lucascs@protonmail.com>
Date: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 14:54:53 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] git: refuse to create a worktree when the repository's config
 can run programs

`git worktree add` checks out a whole tree, and a checkout runs whatever
programs the repository's own config names for it. RealGitRepository::
create_worktree ran a plain `git worktree add`, so a repository whose
.git/config named a filter driver had that program run the moment the
agent panel, the worktree picker or a restored archived thread created a
worktree in it. Repository-local config is not versioned: whoever wrote it
-- a tool, a script, a checkout the user does not trust -- chose the
program, not the user.

The Claude Code VS Code extension refuses this since 2.1.289-2.1.296
(PARITY-REPORT item 105). Its rule, read from the bundled extension.js of
2.1.296 (functions jD1/Py4/DD1 and the refusal messages in Bj1), is:

  - read `git config --local --includes -z --list`, and `--worktree` too;
  - refuse when that cannot be read (non-zero exit other than a missing
    file, more than 1 MiB of output, or output that is not UTF-8), and
    when .git cannot be vetted;
  - refuse `includeIf.*.path`, whose conditional loads cannot be checked;
  - refuse `lfs.customtransfer.*.path`, `lfs.[<url>.]standalonetransferagent`
    and anything under `lfs.extension.`, also when smuggled into a value
    after a newline (Git LFS reads `git config --list` line by line);
  - filter drivers are NOT refused there: it passes
    `-c filter.<name>.clean= -c filter.<name>.smudge=
    -c filter.<name>.process= -c filter.<name>.required=false` to
    `git worktree add`, and refuses only a filter name holding `=` or a
    newline.

This patch mirrors that rule in crates/git/src/worktree_config.rs, run in
create_worktree before anything is written -- not even the parent
directory -- with one deliberate difference: a filter driver with a
non-empty clean/smudge/process command is refused rather than switched
off. A checkout with its filters off writes the raw blobs (an LFS pointer
instead of the file) into a worktree an agent then edits as if it were the
real content; refusing says so instead. An empty command, which is how a
driver is switched off, is accepted.

Not screened, on purpose: `core.fsmonitor`, `core.hooksPath`,
`core.sshCommand`, `credential.helper` and `diff.external`.
GitBinary::build_command already overrides the first on every command and
the rest on every untrusted repository, `git worktree add` included, and
refusing `core.hooksPath` would refuse every husky or lefthook repository
for a setting that is already off where it matters. The extension does not
refuse them either. Its .git vetting (a .git link or gitdir file that does
not point back, a folder borrowing another repository's data) is reduced
here to "the git directory and the common config file must be readable",
checked on the filesystem before git runs, because git's discovery can walk
past an unreadable .git and answer for an enclosing repository.

The error is a typed UnsafeWorktreeConfig whose message names the key
(control characters escaped, long keys shortened) and says what to do:
move the setting to the global config if it is yours, remove it if not.
It reaches the user through the paths that already exist --
worktree_service's "git worktree create failed" toast and its View Log, and
over RPC for a remote project, whose host runs the screening.

Tests: unit tests for each refused class, the multiline smuggling case, a
benign config (a typical clone, include.path, husky hooksPath, builtin
fsmonitor, sshCommand, a switched-off filter, ordinary lfs.* keys), the
listing parser and its limits, and an unreadable .git and config; against
real git, a filter driver in .git/config and one in config.worktree,
includeIf, and an unreadable config are each refused with no directory,
branch or worktree created and the filter never run, and the settings
Zed already overrides still create a worktree.
---

